#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139556} :activity/id #uuid "522ceb8a-6a60-5c1f-99d9-c6421e013ec3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050505 :topic/id #uuid "4ed6acfb-fbd7-5e57-abcc-691c74600c49" :topic/math-academy-id 2278} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139556 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139635 :tutorial/content "Let's look at the three points $A$, $B$, and $C$ below.\n\n![](images/95/956f9b2c979f06897b09119b089ced903e095d72136e64b9ea52e946fba41544.png)\n\nAs indicated in the diagram, the distances between the points are\n\n$$\nAB = 4cm, BC = 2cm, AC = 6cm\n$$\n\nCan we be sure that the points are collinear? To answer this, we use the **segment addition postulate**.\n\nThe postulate states the following:\n\n> *Three points are collinear if and only if the largest distance between two of them is equal to the sum of the other two distances.*\n\nHere, the largest distance is $AC$, so the points $A$, $B$, and $C$ are collinear *if and only if*\n\n$$\nAC=AB + BC\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information\n\n$$\nAC=6\\,\\mathrm{cm}\n$$\n\n$$\nAB=4\\,\\mathrm{cm}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nBC=2\\,\\mathrm{cm}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAC &= AB + BC \\\\\n6 &= 4 + 2 \\\\\n6 &= 6.✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a true statement, so the three points $A$, $B$, and $C$ are indeed collinear.\n\n**Note:** The postulate works in both directions:\n\n- If the points $A$, $B$, and $C$ are collinear then $AC= AB + BC$.\n- If $AC= AB + BC$ then the points $A$, $B$, and $C$ are collinear." :tutorial/id #uuid "0a460529-da55-5fcc-a207-e4ae6a47f12d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2168 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "11ec6bb7-b9b3-51ea-b1b2-5f6a0a7b75b9" :step/math-academy-id 8720 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139557}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139557 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139636 :tutorial/content "Given two of the three distances between three collinear points, we can calculate the third distance using the segment addition postulate.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the collinear points $A$, $B$, and $C$ below.\n\n![](images/3b/3b1bc383e8d570f9dc33346e6f4ae04377e3c5c4c0d0e53abe07b0aacd9bb3e6.png)\n\nWe are given two of the distances:\n\n$$\nAB = 3cm, AC = 5cm\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the third distance, $BC$, using the segment addition postulate.\n\nFirst, we state the equation and substitute the known quantities $AB=3$ and $AC=5$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAC &= AB + BC \\\\\n5 &= 3 + BC\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for $BC$ by subtracting $3$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5 &= 3 + BC \\\\\nBC &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between the points $B$ and $C$ is $2\\,\\textrm{cm}$." :tutorial/id #uuid "92286c39-6af9-5ff0-b2e9-fa44fec8f732" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2169 :tutorial/title "Calculating the Distance Between Collinear Points"} :step/id #uuid "fa4d60ee-75e2-54e2-b7eb-e1946cf89388" :step/math-academy-id 8722 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139558}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139558 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138558 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138282 :question/id #uuid "5b1ee9ef-da39-5f7a-abb5-98149a765b1f" :question/math-academy-id "e-2565"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3108f0ab-17b1-5241-93d1-97658cf22601" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050563}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138768} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138769}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Three Collinear Points"} :step/id #uuid "ab8e5676-8695-5fd6-8d78-6bae4eba68f9" :step/math-academy-id 8723 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139559}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139559 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138559 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138283 :question/id #uuid "cabd7daa-4f22-56ca-8b26-0791857e8eae" :question/math-academy-id "e-2566"} :knowledge-poin